    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Corbin Carroll** (77.03) and **James Wood** (74.75) anchor our lineup as the premier plays. **James Wood** specifically benefits from an elite xwOBA of .430 and a highly favorable park factor (+14%), positioning him to maximize value from the leadoff spot against Kyle Freeland. Meanwhile, **Corbin Carroll** remains an essential start despite the platoon disadvantage; his raw speed and projectable efficiency against Jeffrey Springs keep his score firmly in the \"Strong\" tier. **Tyler Soderstrom** also warrants attention, as the 7.04 xERA of Mitch Bratt combined with the #2 lineup spot pushes him to a 70.82 score, providing significant upside for our Roto categories.\n\nThe most difficult roster decision involved **Austin Riley**, who sits on the bench with a 59.57 score. While **Austin Riley** offers pedigree, his matchup against JP Sears and the reality of a #7 lineup projection kept him just below the threshold required to displace our current starters. We also see high-leverage potential from **Isaac Paredes** (68.21), who benefits from a strong platoon edge against Janson Junk, and **Brice Turang** (66.10), whose matchup against Freddy Peralta is mitigated by an excellent 92% SB-conducive delivery profile, bolstering his value in our scarce stolen base category. \n\nRegarding risks, we are operating under \"Lineup Pending\" status across the board, which introduces variance if any of these projected spots shift downward. While we favor the matchups for **Henry Bolte** (60.22) and **Seiya Suzuki** (64.64), both rely on maintaining favorable order positioning to justify their scores. We are avoiding **Travis Bazzana** (39.94) today, as the matchup against Joe Ryan presents a difficult SP Skill hurdle that suppresses his scoring well below our floor. Monitor weather and late lineup scratches, specifically for **Gabriel Moreno** and **Vinnie Pasquantino**, to ensure the projected output remains intact before the first pitch.",
